  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday Night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1145 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWednesday and Thursday will remain warm overall with continuation\nof temperatures well above-normal. Record highs will also be\npossible on these days assuming clouds or a stray shower/storm\ndon't mess things up. We do have low rain/storm chances on\nWednesday, mainly for areas north of I-40, as a front and \nmoisture axis stretch west-to-east across the Ohio Valley. \nOtherwise, ridging will remain prominent across the Southeast and \nwill influence our very warm temperatures and overall dry \nconditions.\n\nA big cool-down is looming on Friday with the arrival of a polar\ncold front extending from a potent northern stream shortwave \ntrough. Models suggest this front will not coexist with the \naforementioned moisture axis mid week, so rain chances are very \nlow with frontal passage on Friday unless moisture values can\nincrease. Nonetheless, temperatures will make a big drop Friday \nnight into Saturday morning. Lows in the 40s in 50s are forecast \nfor the weekend with upper 30s possible Saturday night across the \nUpper Cumberland.\n\nAt this time there's no meaningful rainfall forecast, or any \nsevere storms, throughout the forecast period. Good news on the\nstorms, but not good news for worsening drought.",
